      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Binge eating disorder (BED) is associated with obesity and major depressive disorder (MDD). Naltrexone extended-release (ER)/bupropion ER (NB) is approved as an adjunct to diet and physical activity for chronic weight management. In a prospectively designed 24-week open-label, single-arm, single-site trial of 25 women with MDD and overweight/obesity, NB reduced weight and depressive symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This post hoc analysis investigated the relationship between change in self-reported binge eating behavior (evaluated with the Binge Eating Scale [BES]) and changes in weight, control of eating, and depressive symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At baseline, 91% of subjects had moderate or severe BES scores, suggesting BED. BES scores were significantly improved from week 4, and by week 24, 83% reported \"little or no problem.\" Improvement in BES scores correlated with improvement in depressive symptoms and control of eating."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "NB may be effective in reducing binge eating symptoms associated with MDD and overweight/obesity. Evaluation of NB in BED appears warranted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "FUNDING",
          "content": "Orexigen Therapeutics, Inc."
        }
      ]},
